## Why This Is Asked

Assess ability to architect a safe, compliant Azure OpenAI deployment that detects bias, enforces tenant isolation, and provides verifiable audit trails.

## Key Concepts

- Grounding with Cognitive Search
- Fairness metrics (disparate impact, equalized odds)
- Prompt design and post-processing
- Per-tenant RBAC and data isolation
- Auditability to Data Lake
- Synthetic bias testing and budget guards

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudocode for post-processing bias flagging
function postProcess(output, ctx) {
  const bias = detectBias(output, ctx.metrics);
  if (bias > THRESH) {
    return { safe: false, mitigations: suggestMitigations(output) };
  }
  return { safe: true, result: output };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you measure bias in production?
- How would you handle false positives in flagged outputs?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests ability to architect a multi-tenant Azure OpenAI flow integrated with DevOps, covering data lineage, security, and performance.

## Key Concepts
- Azure OpenAI + API Management
- Cognitive Search KB in Blob
- Per-tenant RBAC (Azure AD)
- PII redaction (Text Analytics)
- Data Lake logging
- Canary deployments and end-to-end latency goals

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo orchestration snippet showing tenant context, diff->prompt, and moderation
async function reviewPR(prDiff, tenantId){
  const kb = await loadKB(tenantId);
  const prompt = buildPrompt(prDiff, kb, tenantId);
  const review = await openAiReview(prompt);
  if(hasPIIRisk(review)){ redact(review); }
  await storeLogs(tenantId, {prDiff, review});
  return review.comments;
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate and monitor prompt drift across tenants without leaking data?
- What latency and cost controls would you put in place for high-velocity repos?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests end-to-end design: IoT ingress, data governance, model integration, privacy controls, and scalable user access governance. It probes architectural decisions, trade-offs, and verification strategies.

## Key Concepts

- Azure IoT Hub ingestion and event-driven processing
- Data Lake Gen2 storage for raw and structured telemetry
- Vector store for embeddings and scheduled refresh cadence
- API Management with per-tenant quotas and private networking to Azure OpenAI
- Purview for data lineage and governance
- Data residency, PII redaction, and security testing

## Code Example

```javascript
// Node.js snippet: per-tenant quota check in Azure Functions
module.exports = async function (context, req) {
  const tenant = req.headers['x-tenant-id'];
  // fetch quota from a store (e.g., Cosmos Redis cache)
  const quota = await getQuota(tenant);
  if (quota.remaining <= 0) {
    context.res = { status: 429, body: 'Quota exhausted' };
    return;
  }
  await consumeOne(tenant);
  context.res = { status: 200, body: 'ok' };
};
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you test latency under burst IoT traffic while preserving data residency?
- What strategies ensure embeddings stay up-to-date without leaking sensitive telemetry to the model?

## Why This Is Asked
This tests real-world infra decisions around data residency, confidential inference, and auditable prompts in a regulated fintech context.

## Key Concepts
- Azure OpenAI in Confidential Computing
- Multi-tenant isolation (RBAC, per-tenant caches)
- Data residency controls and API Management routing
- PII masking and prompt/log redaction
- Audit trails to Data Lake and cost governance

## Code Example
```javascript
// Example: redact and route to OpenAI within tenant boundaries
function preparePrompt(input, tenantId){
  const redacted = redactPII(input);
  const region = getTenantRegion(tenantId);
  if(region !== 'allowed-region') throw new Error('Region violation');
  return callOpenAI(redacted, {tenantId});
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate end-to-end latency under peak load?
- What tests would verify region-boundary enforcement and audit completeness?
